[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 5, "caption": "Fig. 1 Overview of Shusi. a, Pipeline for constructing single-cell protein interaction networks from scRNA-seq data. b, Architecture of the Shusi model for predicting novel interactions on single cell level. d, Fine-tuning and application of Shusi to AML patient scRNA-seq data.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific workflow figure with panels labeled **a**, **b**, and **c**.\n\n**Panel a: Data Collection & Processing**\n- Shows inputs for network construction:\n  - **23 cancer types / 234 cell lines** illustrated with human organ/cancer-related icons.\n  - **12,350 proteins** illustrated by a protein ribbon structure.\n  - **scRNA-seq** shown as a t-SNE scatter plot with colored cell clusters.\n  - **Experimentally validated network (STRING-E)** shown as a node-link graph.\n- Transformation arrows indicate combining scRNA-seq data and STRING-E into a stack of constructed networks.\n- Output labeled **75,010 Networks** under **Network Construction**.\n\n**Panel b: Model Training**\n- Left side labeled **Large Language Model**.\n- Inputs include:\n  - **Amino acid sequence** represented by sequence/structure icons.\n  - **Functional description** represented by molecular/annotation icons.\n- These pass through an **LLM** block to produce **Protein Embeddings**.\n- Protein embeddings are used to create **Network per cell**, with example cell-specific graphs labeled **Cell 1**, **Cell 2**, **Cell 3**, and **Cell N**.\n- Right side labeled **Variational Graph Autoencode**.\n- Shows an edge feature formula: **Edgeᵢ = Eᵢ * gᵢ * Iᵢ**.\n- Cell-specific networks feed into a **Graph Isomorphism Network** with encoder, latent variable **Z**, and decoder.\n- Output task is **Link Prediction**, shown as predicted node-link graphs for multiple cells.\n\n**Panel c: Drug Target Identification**\n- Shows a **Shusi Model** fine-tuning workflow using **AML Patient scRNA-seq**, represented by a t-SNE plot and patient icons.\n- Fine-tuned model produces graph/link prediction outputs grouped into colored clusters.\n- These feed into **Target Identification**, shown as a molecular network with drug icons.\n- Final step is **Experimental Validation**, illustrated by a human body/cancer icon and a multiwell plate.\n\nOverall, the figure depicts a computational biology pipeline integrating cancer cell line data, protein information, scRNA-seq, experimentally validated protein networks, language-model-derived protein embeddings, and a variational graph autoencoder/GIN model for cell-specific link prediction and drug target identification, followed by experimental validation."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Fig. 2 Evaluation of Shusi's performance. a, Datasets used for model training. The bar plot shows the distribution of cell lines across 23 cancer types. Box plots (left to right) depict: (i) the number of cells per cell line, (ii) the number of genes per training network, and (iii) the number of edges per training network. b, Comparison of model parameters between Shusi and baseline methods. c, Precision of edges predicted by Shusi and baseline models compared to experimentally validated interactions in STRING-E. d, Workflow of the ablation study evaluating node embedding contributions. e, Top 10,000 precision scores from the node embedding ablation study. f, Workflow of the ablation study evaluating edge embedding contributions. g, Top 10,000 precision scores from the edge embedding ablation study. h. Validation scores between novel edges predicted by Shusi and experimentally detected interactions. i, Validation scores comparing interactions in STRING-E with experimentally detected interactions. j, Validation scores comparing Shusi-predicted interactions with experimentally detected interactions. k, l, Cell-specific validation scores comparing Shusi-predicted interactions with experimentally detected interactions for MCF7 (k) and MDAMB231 (l) cell lines. m, Validation scores between novel edges predicted by Shusi and interactions in the integrated network. n, Validation scores comparing interactions in STRING-E with those in the integrated network. o, Cell-specific validation scores comparing Shusi-predicted interactions with interactions in the integrated network. 7", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure labeled `a` through `o`, focused on cell-line or tissue network prediction/validation and feature ablation.\n\nVisible content:\n- `a`: Three grouped box/bar-style summaries across many tissue types. Left axis lists tissues such as autonomic ganglia, biliary tract, bladder, bone, breast, central nervous system, cervix, endometrium, kidney, large intestine, liver, lung, oesophagus, ovary, pancreas, pleura, prostate, skin, soft tissue, stomach, thyroid, upper aerodigestive tract, urinary tract. Plots summarize `#Cell lines`, `#Cells in Each Cell lines`, `#Genes`, and `#Edges`.\n- `b`: Bar chart comparing deep learning models by `#Parameters (M)`. Models include GCN, GraphSAGE, GAT, VGAE, VoGAE, and Shusi.\n- `c`: Scatter/lollipop-style comparison of model `Precision` versus deep learning model, with Shusi visibly much higher than the others.\n- `d`: Schematic model interface. Inputs include a gene/sequence-like object, amino acid sequence, and functional description. Amino acid sequence is embedded using `Galactica`; functional description is embedded using `Sentence-BERT`; features are combined through `Feature Fusion`.\n- `e`: Ablation bar chart of feature embedding types, including nucleotide ID, amino acid sequence, functional annotation, and feature fusion. Metric is `Top 10,000 Precision (%)`.\n- `f`: Schematic showing gene/protein-like entities with expression terms `E_i`, `E_j`, edge scoring without expression versus with expression. With expression uses a multiplicative expression-based edge score.\n- `g`: Ablation bar chart comparing `Without expression` and `With expression`, again using `Top 10,000 Precision (%)`.\n- `h`: Network overlap diagram comparing an experimental network of cell line A and a predicted network of cell line A, with a labeled validation score.\n- `i`: Bar chart of validation score for experimental AP-MS networks, including MCF7 10X and MDAMB231 BGI; a red dashed baseline is shown.\n- `j`: Bar chart of validation score for cell lines predicted by Shusi, including MCF7 10X, MCF7 BGI, and MDAMB231 BGI.\n- `k`: Histogram of validation scores per cell for MCF7 10X.\n- `l`: Histogram of validation scores per cell for MDAMB231 BGI.\n- `m`: Network integration schematic showing predicted network per cell integrated with external networks such as BioPlex 2.0, Hu.MAP, HI-II-14, BioGrid, and MS-based networks, producing a validation score.\n- `n`: Bar chart showing validation score for `STRING-E`, with a red dashed baseline.\n- `o`: Violin plots of validation scores across tissues, using the same tissue categories as panel `a`; a red dashed baseline is shown.\n\nBiological/source objects visible:\n- Tissue categories from cancer or cell-line datasets.\n- Cell lines including MCF7 and MDAMB231.\n- Gene/protein interaction networks, expression values, amino acid sequences, and functional annotations.\n- External protein interaction/reference networks including BioPlex, Hu.MAP, HI-II-14, BioGrid, STRING-E, and MS-based networks.\n\nOverall finding shown visually:\n- The model named `Shusi` has far more parameters and substantially higher precision than other graph/deep-learning baselines.\n- Feature fusion outperforms individual feature embeddings.\n- Including expression information improves top-ranked precision.\n- Predicted cell-line/cell networks show validation above red dashed baselines across AP-MS/reference-network comparisons and across tissues."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 10, "caption": "Fig. 3 Evaluation of novel edges detected by Shusi. a, Bar plot shows the number of edges in Shusipredicted novel networks across cell lines. b, Bar plot shows the number of nodes in Shusi-predicted novel networks across cell lines. c, UMAP visualizes the degree matrix across single cells, highlighting cell-type-specific network topologies. d, Bar plot shows the percentage of marker genes included in Shusi-predicted novel networks. e, Enrichment analysis of cancer hallmarks for nodes in Shusipredicted novel networks compared to marker genes. f, Top: Bar plot shows the number of mutationharboring genes per cancer type. Bottom: Boxplot compares the enrichment of mutation-harboring genes in Shusi-predicted novel networks versus marker genes across cancer types. NA represents that no driver gene has been recorded in the CCLE database. g, Comparison of the percentage of driver genes detected in Shusi-predicted novel networks versus those identified as marker genes. h, List of driver genes identified in Shusi-predicted novel networks across 23 cancer types.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure labeled **a-h** showing pan-tissue/cell-line network and marker-gene analyses.\n\nVisible content:\n- **a**: Circular chord/ring plot labeled **“Edges Across Cell line”** with center text **“Log(#Edges)”**. Outer segments are color-coded by tissue/cancer type, with a legend listing tissues such as autonomic ganglia, biliary tract, blood, bone, breast, CNS, cervix, endometrium, kidney, large intestine, liver, lung, oesophagus, ovary, pancreas, pleura, prostate, skin, soft tissue, stomach, thyroid, upper aerodigestive tract, and urinary tract.\n- **b**: Similar circular plot labeled **“Nodes Across Cell line”** with center text **“Log(#Nodes)”**, again color-coded by tissue type.\n- **c**: t-SNE scatterplot labeled **“Degree Matrix”**, axes **tSNE1** and **tSNE2**, with points colored by tissue/cancer type.\n- **d**: Bar plot labeled **“% Marker Gene involving in Networks”**, x-axis **Cell lines**, colored by tissue group.\n- **e**: Horizontal bar chart comparing **Network Gene** and **Marker Gene** counts across **Cancer Hallmarks**. Hallmarks include PI3K AKT MTOR signaling, oxidative phosphorylation, DNA repair, reactive oxygen species pathway, TGF beta signaling, MYC targets, adipogenesis, unfolded protein response, UV response, WNT beta catenin signaling, fatty acid metabolism, androgen response, MTORC1 signaling, E2F targets, G2M checkpoint, mitotic spindle, apoptosis, P53 pathway, estrogen response, epithelial mesenchymal transition, TNFA signaling via NFKB, protein secretion, notch signaling, peroxisome, glycolysis, hypoxia, interferon gamma response, coagulation, KRAS signaling, angiogenesis, apical surface, complement, IL2 STAT5 signaling, inflammatory response, and myogenesis. X-axis is **#Cell lines**.\n- **f**: Tissue-wise mutation enrichment plot. Top bars show counts per tissue, with labels such as **NA**, **ns**, and significance stars. Lower panel shows box/bar-style values labeled **Mutation enrichment -Log(P Value)**, comparing **Network Gene** and **Marker Gene**.\n- **g**: Bar chart labeled **%Driver Gene overlap**, comparing overlap for **Network Gene** and **Marker Gene** across tissue categories.\n- **h**: Dot/bubble plot of **Driver gene** versus **Tissue**, where dot size encodes **#Celllines**. Driver genes listed include **TP63, TP53, TOP2B, STL, SMARCA4, SCARA, RB1, RAC1, POLE, PIK3R1, PIK3CA, PATL1, NRAS, MYO10, MTOR, MLLT10, MAX, MAP2K1, KRAS, HRAS, GATA3, FBXW7, EGFR, DUSP16, DNAJA4, DIS3, CTNNB1, CDKN2A, BRAF, ATRX, ARID1A, ANKRD40, ACTB10**.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Human cancer cell lines grouped by tissue/cancer type.\n- Gene networks, marker genes, network genes, mutation-enriched genes, and known cancer driver genes.\n- Cancer hallmark gene sets/pathways.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Network-derived edge and node summaries across cell lines.\n- Degree matrix projected into t-SNE space.\n- Enrichment analyses using mutation data and cancer hallmark/pathway categories.\n- Overlap analysis with driver genes.\n- Dot-size encoding of number of cell lines per tissue-driver-gene combination.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Network edges and nodes vary across tissue/cell-line groups.\n- Degree matrix clusters show tissue-colored structure in t-SNE.\n- Marker-gene network involvement differs strongly across cell lines.\n- Marker genes and network genes show different representation across cancer hallmark categories.\n- Mutation enrichment and driver-gene overlap vary by tissue, with several prominent enrichments in lung, skin, urinary tract, and other tissues."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 12, "caption": "Fig. 4 Evaluation of dependency probabilities for nodes in novel networks. a, Workflow illustrates the collection of dependency probabilities from the CCLE and the computation of node degrees and cross-cell recurrence ratios based on network topologies. b, Heatmap shows the percentage of genes with dependency probabilities > 0 . 5 across different degree centrality groups. c, Distribution of dependency probabilities across different degree centrality groups. d, Pipeline for selecting the filtered cell-type-specific gene list and the randomly sampled gene list. e, Boxplot comparing dependency probabilities between the filtered cell-type-specific gene list and the randomly sampled gene list.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure about predicting and evaluating cell-line-specific gene dependency from single-cell networks and DepMap CRISPR-Cas9 screening.\n\nPanel a: Schematic comparing DepMap cell lines and genes with model-predicted cell lines and genes. Venn diagrams show overlap between “Cell lines in DepMap” and “Cell lines in model,” and between “Genes in DepMap” and “Genes predicted by model.” A gene effect distribution from CRISPR-Cas9 screening is transformed into a dependency probability matrix with rows labeled Gene 1, Gene 2, Gene 11,763 and columns labeled Cellline 1 through Cellline 186. At right, a “Single Cell Network Predicted by Shusi” is evaluated through cell-line-specific degree and cross-cell recurrence network metrics.\n\nPanel b: Heatmap of “Cell-line-specific Degree” across tissues. Rows are grouped by tissue type with a color-coded tissue legend including autonomic ganglia, biliary tract, blood, bone, breast, central nervous system, endometrium, kidney, large intestine, liver, lung, oesophagus, ovary, pancreas, pleura, prostate, skin, soft tissue, stomach, thyroid, upper aerodigestive tract, and urinary tract. Columns are degree bins from 0-250 to >750. Color encodes percentage dependency probability >0.5.\n\nPanel c: Faceted density/ridge plots by tissue showing dependency probability distributions across cell-line-specific degree bins. X-axis is dependency probability, y-axis is cell-line-specific degree category. Curves are colored by dependency probability scale from low to high.\n\nPanel d: Workflow schematic from scRNA-seq data to model prediction, single-cell network, recurrence filtering, cell-type-specific network, degree filtering, and filtered cell-type-specific gene list. A comparison box shows random sampling gene list generation.\n\nPanel e: Boxplots comparing dependency probability distributions across tissues for “Network Gene” versus “Random Sampling.” Network genes show consistently higher dependency probabilities than random sampling across tissue categories, with significance markers above many comparisons."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 14, "caption": "Fig. 5 Performance of identifying drug target genes in novel networks. a, Workflow for classifying drugs into highand intermediate-responsiveness groups for each cell line. The bar plot shows the number of drug target genes for high- and intermediate-responsiveness drugs across cell lines. b, Boxplot comparing the percentage of high- and intermediate-responsiveness drug targets identified in Shusi-predicted networks. c, Boxplot comparing the degree centrality of high- and intermediate-responsiveness drug targets in Shusi-predicted networks. d, Boxplot comparing the crosscell recurrence of high- and intermediate-responsiveness drug targets in Shusi-predicted networks.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical data figure labeled `a-d`.\n\nPanel `a`: Schematic and waterfall/bar plot. The schematic shows cell line counts in a model and GDSC, with overlapping counts `89`, `143`, and `857`, followed by drug screening using `LN(IC50)` and categorization into `High Responsiveness` and `Intermediate Responsiveness` using thresholds shown as `< -2 µM` and `> 1 µM`. The bar plot ranks cell lines by response, with positive and negative bars colored by tissue type. A tissue legend includes many cancer/tissue sources such as autonomic ganglia, biliary tract, blood, bone, breast, central nervous system, cervix, endometrium, kidney, large intestine, liver, lung, oesophagus, ovary, pancreas, pleura, prostate, skin, soft tissue, stomach, thyroid, upper aerodigestive tract, and urinary tract.\n\nPanel `b`: Boxplots comparing `%CircRNA identified in networks per cellline` across tissues. Two response groups are shown: red for high responsiveness and yellow for intermediate responsiveness. Statistical annotations above tissues include `ns`, `*`, `**`, and `***`.\n\nPanel `c`: Boxplots comparing `Cell-line-specific Degree per Gene` across tissues for the same two response groups. The y-axis extends to roughly 2000, with many outlier points. Statistical significance labels are shown above tissue categories.\n\nPanel `d`: Boxplots comparing `Cross-cell occurrence per Gene` across tissues for high versus intermediate responsiveness. The y-axis ranges approximately 0 to 0.8, with many outliers and significance labels.\n\nBiological source objects: cancer cell lines grouped by tissue of origin; genes; circRNAs/networks; drug response categories based on LN(IC50).\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: cell lines are filtered/intersected between model and GDSC, drug response is thresholded into responsiveness groups, and network/gene metrics are summarized by tissue and response type using boxplots.\n\nVisible finding: across tissue types, high- and intermediate-responsiveness cell lines differ in circRNA network identification rates, cell-line-specific gene degree, and cross-cell gene occurrence, with significance varying by tissue."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 17, "caption": "Fig. 6 Network-based characterization of AML cellular hierarchy. a, Workflow diagram of Shusi model fine-tuning using primary AML specimens. b, tSNE visualization of degree matrices across 14 cell types. c, d, tSNE projections of primitive (c) and monocytic (d) cellular subsets based on network degrees. e, Visualization of the mono-specific network highlighting significantly enriched cancer hallmarks (text labels and dashed circles) and drug target genes (diamond-shaped nodes). Node sizes scale with degrees. f, Degree distribution of genes in the monocyte-specific network. g, A schematic diagram describing the heterogeneity and predicted drug responses of the MMP sample. h, Gating strategy for determining the primitive and the monocytic subsets of the MMP sample. i-l, tSNE analysis of immunophenotypic data of residual live cells after drug treatment. The dot plots (top two rows) display the absolute cell number and the pseudo plots (bottom two rows) display the relative cell density. The percentages are calculated by multiplying relative cell viability with the proportion of primitive (blue) or monocytic (red) subsets. 17", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure about AML single-cell network analysis and drug response.\n\nVisible content:\n- Panel a: Workflow schematic. Starts with “27 Patient Samples” and “32,146 scRNA-seq”, shown as a tSNE/UMAP-like cell embedding. A model/interface labeled “SHUSI” is fine-tuned, producing “Single Cell Protein Networks”. These are converted into a “Single Cell Degree Matrix” with rows Gene 1, Gene 2, Gene N and columns Cell 1 through Cell 32,146. The matrix is summarized into “Cell-type Specific Networks”.\n- Panel b: tSNE plot labeled “AML single cell network #Cells=32,146”. Cells are colored by annotated AML and immune cell categories, including LSPC-Cycle, LSPC-Primed, LSPC-Quiescent, Mono-like, ProMono-like, GMP-like, MEP, B, CD4 T, CD8 T, cDC-like, NK, Plasma, and unknown.\n- Panel c: tSNE plot labeled “Venetoclax-sensitive Primitive subsets #Cells=9,052”. Shows three primitive LSPC groups: LSPC-Cycle, LSPC-Primed, and LSPC-Quiescent.\n- Panel d: tSNE plot labeled “Venetoclax-resistant Monocytic subsets #Cells=10,741”. Shows ProMono-like and Mono-like subsets.\n- Panel e: Large “Mono-specific Network” graph. Nodes and edges form a dense protein/gene interaction-style network. Highlighted genes include JAK2, RARA, MAP2K1, LYN, HCK, FGR, FYN, SYK, and PTPN6. Labeled pathway/module regions include MTORC1 SIGNALING, TGF BETA SIGNALING, TNFA SIGNALING VIA NFKB, MYC TARGETS V1, PI3K AKT MTOR SIGNALING, ALLOGRAFT REJECTION, and UV RESPONSE UP.\n- Panel f: Rank plot of “Degree of Mono-specific Network” versus rank. A threshold line is labeled “RANK = 75 (Top 10%)”. High-ranking genes labeled near the top include JAK2, HCK, RARA, LYN, SYK, FYN, FGR, PTPN6, and MAP2K1.\n- Panel g: Experimental schematic for AML patient MMP sample. Mixed monocytic and primitive subsets are divided into “Ven-sen Prim subsets” and “Ven-res Mono subsets”, then treated with Ven/Aza, Drug X, or VA+X.\n- Panel h: tSNE analysis and gating strategy. A schematic gate separates Ven-sensitive primitive subsets marked CD34+/CD117+ from Ven-resistant monocytic subsets marked CD14+/CD11b+. Four marker expression plots show CD34, CD14, CD117, and CD11b with high-to-low color scales.\n- Panels i-l: Flow cytometry-like scatter/density plots comparing Control and +VA conditions, with dashed gates and percentages. Columns are labeled:\n  - i: Control\n  - j: JAK2 + Fedratinib\n  - k: RARA + Tamibarotene\n  - l: PTPN6 (SHP1) + SC-43\n  Lower rows show density-style plots with a high-to-low color scale.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- AML patient samples\n- Single-cell RNA-seq-derived cells\n- AML cell subsets, including primitive LSPC subsets and monocytic/pro-monocytic subsets\n- Protein/gene network nodes and pathway modules\n- Marker-defined cell populations using CD34, CD117, CD14, and CD11b\n\nMain depicted finding:\n- The figure links SHUSI-derived single-cell protein networks to AML cell-type-specific networks, highlighting a monocytic/venetoclax-resistant network with candidate genes such as JAK2, RARA, and PTPN6 and showing experimental drug-combination response/gating analyses for monocytic versus primitive AML subsets."]}]

